<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <!--
    Shared application manifest for MTEngineSDL host apps. Import
    MTEngineSDLHost.props from your .vcxproj and the linker embeds this.

    activeCodePage=UTF-8 makes GetACP() return 65001 for the whole process,
    which is what every *narrow* Win32/CRT entry point uses to turn a char*
    path into the wide name the filesystem actually stores: fopen, fopen_s,
    std::ifstream, std::filesystem's std::string constructors, CreateFileA,
    FindFirstFileA, and argv.

    MTEngineSDL's convention is that a path in a char* or std::string is
    UTF-8 (native on macOS and Linux). Without this setting those UTF-8 bytes
    get decoded as CP1252 / CP1250 / CP932, so any file under a folder or with
    a name outside the machine's ANSI code page fails to open: Polish or other
    Latin-Extended names, CJK, emoji, macOS NFD normalized accents on files
    that came from another OS. The wide APIs list such a file happily and the
    narrow open then reports "no such file", which reads as data corruption or
    a missing file rather than an encoding bug.

    It cannot be fixed call site by call site alone: the affected calls are
    spread across the engine and its vendored third party libraries. The
    engine's own media loading path is ALSO hardened in code (see
    src/Engine/Core/SYS_FileUtf8.h) so it works even without this manifest,
    but the manifest is what fixes everything else at once.

    Requires Windows 10 1903 (build 18362) or newer. Older Windows ignores the
    setting and behaves exactly as before.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
